A||||8||Howard_Sacks||Bump Stop Trimming Questions||||5||
Z||000000||Howard_Sacks||12-18-2001||02:27 PM||||I've read a lot and heard from people that if you're lowering the car using springs, that you need to trim the bump stops.<P> I currently have my front control arms off of the car and am pressing in fresh bushings tomorrow. When I reinstall the arms, I'm going to use Eiback "prokit" springs. Could someone explain the process of trimming the bumpstops to me if it's necessary to do so?<P>or Are there instructions for doing this anywhere?||||reg||5
Z||000001||80's BOY||12-18-2001||02:44 PM||||well, being that i was big into the mini-truck scene, i'll share how we used to do it.<P>heat up an old kitchen knife or large knife with some weight to it until it glows red. then you can slice right through the bumpstop. we usually only cut half off of the bump but each application varies. good luck. is there really any other way to do it??||||reg||14
Z||000002||DKOV||12-18-2001||06:46 PM||||Sawz-All?  Motorized Bread Knife?  Rotory tool with 3 inch cut off wheel?  Hack saw?  Air grinder?<P>It all sounds like so much fun!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000003||shark93726||12-18-2001||08:34 PM||||If you can cut the rubber stop so there is a triangular piece left sticking down, it will still soften the blow when you encounter hard bumps, which helps to keep from bending suspension parts.<P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000004||West Coast Fiero||12-18-2001||08:53 PM||||on Pre-88 applications you want to take a saw-zall and remove the bumpstop mount by cutting off the bumbstop perch 2 1/2" up from the bump stop and remove 3/4" from the perch you just cut off, then reweld it back onto the vehicle. This will yeild the same bumpstop location in perportion with the lowering springs.<P> As far as the rear, simply take the upper bumpstop plate, and turn it upside down ( some grinding arouund the curcumference of the bumpstop protrusion on the plate will be neccessary for it to slip back through the vehicle's chassis.<P> for further drtails on these modifications, call WCF.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||Howard_Sacks||12-19-2001||12:01 PM||||Thanks Eric,  I couldn't imagine that I was supposed to trim the already thin rubber part of the bump stop.  That would make it metal to metal contact.<P>Do you think I could get away with mig welding it back or do you think it needs to be tig welded?<P>For those who have never taken off their wheels, unlike other cars, there isn't a whole lot of rubber:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.howard.saturnet.net/fiero_jpgs/bumpstop.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Howard_Sacks (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||12-19-2001||12:20 PM||||MIG welded undoubtably, if its good enough for the boys in NASCAR, its good enough for the rest of us<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||12-19-2001||02:14 PM||||the eibach kit isn't enough to need to trim the bump stops. i had them on my 86 and did lots of stupid driving and never once bottomed out the suspension. my cars droped 2.5" now and all i did was shave 3/4" off and i still don't hit em. <P>so don't waste your time. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-19-2001||03:28 PM||||Ive had mine bottom before and its all unaltered OEM suspension. Only diff from stock is Bilstien shocks.||||reg||1
